/* Bassiet book, Depth 6: The Apothecary Drifts. Original text. */
window.BOOK=window.BOOK||{};
BOOK['c6-pk']={depth:6,title:'The Currents',
epi:'Everything given topside drifts down here. Watch how it moves, where it pools and how long it stays.',
c:['pharmacokinetics','digoxin','antibiotics'],
S:[
{h:'Absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion',p:[
'**Bioavailability (F)** is the fraction of a dose that reaches the systemic circulation unchanged: 100% for IV, less for oral drugs because of incomplete absorption and **first-pass metabolism** in the gut wall and liver. Drugs with heavy first-pass loss (oral nitroglycerin, morphine, propranolol, lidocaine) need much larger oral doses or another route; sublingual, transdermal and rectal routes partly bypass the portal circulation.',
'Only the **un-ionised** form of a drug crosses lipid membranes easily. A weak acid is mostly un-ionised where the pH is below its pKa, and a weak base where the pH is above its pKa (the Henderson-Hasselbalch relationship). This decides where drugs are absorbed and where they are trapped: an ionised molecule cannot diffuse back, so a weak acid gathers in alkaline urine and a weak base in acidic fluid.',
'**Volume of distribution** relates the amount in the body to the plasma concentration: **Vd = amount in body / plasma concentration**. It is a ratio, not a real space. Only the free, unbound drug acts, diffuses and is filtered; albumin carries mainly acidic drugs (warfarin, phenytoin), and α₁-acid glycoprotein mainly basic ones. When albumin falls (nephrotic syndrome, cirrhosis, malnutrition), the free fraction of a highly bound drug rises, so the same total level can be toxic.'],
table:{cols:['Vd','Where the drug sits','Examples'],rows:[
['about 3–5 L (plasma)','large, charged or tightly protein-bound molecules stay in vessels','heparin, warfarin, monoclonal antibodies'],
['about 12–15 L (extracellular fluid)','small water-soluble molecules that cannot enter cells','aminoglycosides, mannitol'],
['about 40 L (total body water)','small molecules that cross membranes freely','ethanol'],
['hundreds of litres','lipophilic drugs bound in fat, muscle or other tissue','digoxin, amiodarone, chloroquine, TCAs']]},
s:[['phys','Dialysis removes a drug well only when it is small, water-soluble, weakly protein-bound and has a low Vd, so most of it is in the plasma passing the filter. That is why salicylates, lithium, toxic alcohols and metformin are dialysable in overdose, and digoxin and TCAs are not.']]},
{h:'Reaching and holding a level',fig:'pk',p:[
'**Clearance** is the volume of plasma cleared of drug per unit time: **CL = rate of elimination / plasma concentration**. Half-life depends on both clearance and distribution: **t½ = 0.693 × Vd / CL**, so a big Vd or a low clearance lengthens it. With repeated doses or a constant infusion, the drug accumulates until the amount eliminated per interval equals the amount given. That balance is **steady state**, and it is reached after about 4 to 5 half-lives (94–97%) whatever the dose: a bigger dose raises the plateau but does not reach it sooner.',
'A **loading dose** fills the volume of distribution at once, so it depends on Vd and does not change in kidney or liver failure. A **maintenance dose** replaces what is cleared, so it depends on clearance and must fall when clearance falls. Longer dosing intervals give wider swings between peak and trough; a continuous infusion gives no swing at all.'],
table:{cols:['Equation','Use'],rows:[
['Loading dose = (Cp × Vd) / F','reach target fast (unaffected by kidney or liver failure)'],
['Maintenance dose = (Cp × CL × τ) / F','keep the target (lower in kidney or liver failure)'],
['Infusion rate = Css × CL','the steady-state level of an infusion is set by rate and clearance'],
['t½ = 0.693 × Vd / CL','time to halve the concentration'],
['Steady state ≈ 4 to 5 half-lives','with regular dosing or infusion, regardless of dose']]},
key:'Steady state takes 4–5 half-lives no matter how much you give. A loading dose gets there faster.'},
{h:'Orders of elimination',p:[
'Most drugs follow **first-order** kinetics: a constant **fraction** is removed per unit time, so the half-life is constant and the concentration falls exponentially (a straight line on a log scale). When the elimination enzymes are saturated, drugs follow **zero-order** kinetics: a constant **amount** is removed per unit time, the concentration falls in a straight line on an ordinary scale, the half-life lengthens as the level rises, and a small dose increase can cause a large jump in level. Three drugs to know reach saturation at ordinary or overdose amounts: **phenytoin, ethanol and high-dose aspirin**. Picture a drain already running at full bore: pour in more and the level only rises.',
'The kidney clears drugs in three ways. **Filtration** handles only the unbound fraction. **Active secretion** in the proximal tubule uses organic anion transporters (penicillins, methotrexate, loop and thiazide diuretics; probenecid competes with them) and organic cation transporters (metformin; cimetidine and trimethoprim compete, which also raises creatinine without harming filtration). **Reabsorption** recovers un-ionised, lipid-soluble drug, which is why urine pH changes excretion.'],
s:[['phys','Weak acids (aspirin, phenobarbital, methotrexate) are trapped in alkaline urine: give sodium bicarbonate to speed their excretion. Weak bases (amphetamines, TCAs) are trapped in acidic urine, though acidifying urine is rarely used now.']]},
{h:'The liver\'s two phases',p:[
'**Phase I** (cytochrome P450, mostly CYP3A4, 2D6, 2C9, 2C19, 1A2) oxidises, reduces or hydrolyses drugs, often to active or toxic metabolites. Older adults lose phase I function first. **Phase II** conjugates (glucuronidation, sulfation, acetylation, glutathione) to make drugs water-soluble and inactive; phase II is relatively preserved with age, so benzodiazepines cleared only by conjugation (lorazepam, oxazepam, temazepam) are safer in older people and in liver disease. **Slow acetylators** (N-acetyltransferase) build up isoniazid (neuropathy), hydralazine and procainamide (drug-induced lupus).',
'Induction takes days to weeks, because new enzyme protein has to be made, and it fades just as slowly after the inducer stops. Inhibition starts within a day, because the drug simply competes for or blocks the enzyme already there. A warfarin dose that is right while a patient takes rifampin becomes dangerous weeks after rifampin is stopped.'],
table:{cols:['P450 inducers (levels of other drugs fall)','P450 inhibitors (levels rise)'],rows:[
['carbamazepine, phenytoin, phenobarbital, rifampin, St John\'s wort, griseofulvin, chronic alcohol, smoking (CYP1A2), modafinil','macrolides (not azithromycin), azoles, protease inhibitors (ritonavir), grapefruit juice, cimetidine, amiodarone, isoniazid, valproate, fluoxetine and paroxetine (2D6), ciprofloxacin (1A2), acute alcohol']]},
s:[['pharm','Classic victims: warfarin (bleeding when inhibitors are added), ciclosporin and tacrolimus, theophylline, oral contraceptives (failure with inducers), statins (myopathy with CYP3A4 inhibitors), clopidogrel (needs CYP2C19 to activate; poor metabolisers get less effect).'],['bio','Genes change drug handling. CYP2D6 ultrarapid metabolisers turn codeine into too much morphine; poor metabolisers get no pain relief. HLA-B*57:01 predicts abacavir hypersensitivity and HLA-B*15:02 (common in South-East Asian ancestry) carbamazepine-induced Stevens-Johnson syndrome, so both are tested before starting. Low TPMT or NUDT15 activity makes azathioprine and mercaptopurine toxic to the marrow; DPD deficiency does the same for 5-fluorouracil; CYP2C9 and VKORC1 variants change warfarin dose.']]},
{h:'Special people',p:[
'**Kidney disease**: cut maintenance doses of renally cleared drugs (aminoglycosides, vancomycin, digoxin, lithium, many antibiotics, DOACs, metformin, enoxaparin). **Liver disease**: less metabolism, lower albumin and therefore more free drug, and portosystemic shunts that let oral drugs skip first-pass extraction. **Elderly**: lower kidney function, more fat, less water and albumin; lipophilic drugs such as diazepam have a longer half-life. **Pregnancy**: higher Vd and clearance; most drugs cross the placenta, and the embryo is most vulnerable to malformation during organogenesis (weeks 3 to 8 after conception), while later exposure tends to affect growth and function.'],
table:{cols:['Teratogen','Effect'],rows:[['ACE inhibitors, ARBs','fetal kidney damage, oligohydramnios, skull hypoplasia (2nd and 3rd trimesters)'],['Isotretinoin, high-dose vitamin A','craniofacial, heart and CNS defects; two forms of contraception required'],['Valproate','neural tube defects (spina bifida), lower IQ'],['Carbamazepine','neural tube defects, craniofacial defects, fingernail hypoplasia'],['Phenytoin','fetal hydantoin syndrome (cleft lip and palate, hypoplastic nails and digits)'],['Warfarin','nasal and bone hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses, fetal haemorrhage (use heparin)'],['Lithium','Ebstein anomaly'],['Methotrexate','miscarriage, skull and limb defects'],['Mycophenolate','miscarriage, ear, lip and palate malformations'],['Methimazole','aplasia cutis and choanal atresia (use propylthiouracil in the first trimester)'],['High-dose fluconazole','craniofacial and limb defects'],['Tetracyclines','teeth discolouration, impaired bone growth'],['Aminoglycosides','ototoxicity (VIII nerve)'],['Diethylstilbestrol','vaginal clear cell adenocarcinoma and uterine anomalies in daughters'],['Thalidomide','limb defects (phocomelia)'],['Alcohol','fetal alcohol syndrome: smooth philtrum, thin upper lip, short palpebral fissures, microcephaly, heart defects; the leading preventable cause of intellectual disability']]}}
],
Q:[
{q:'A drug given every 8 hours has a half-life of 6 hours. Roughly how long after starting will its level reach steady state?',o:['About 6 hours','About 12 hours','About 24–30 hours','About 72 hours'],a:2,x:'Steady state comes after 4 to 5 half-lives, here 24 to 30 hours, whatever the size of each dose or the dosing interval. The dose decides the height of the plateau, not when it is reached; only a loading dose gets there sooner.',d:'pharm',c:'pharmacokinetics'},
{q:'A man whose creatinine clearance has fallen to a third of normal needs gentamicin for pyelonephritis. How should the regimen change?',o:['Lower the loading dose and keep the maintenance dose','Keep the loading dose and lower the maintenance dose','Keep both doses and shorten the interval','Raise the loading dose to overcome poor filtration'],a:1,x:'The loading dose fills the volume of distribution, which kidney failure does not change, so it stays the same. Maintenance replaces what is cleared, and gentamicin is cleared by filtration, so the dose must fall or the interval lengthen. Shortening the interval would make accumulation and ototoxicity worse.',d:'pharm',c:'pharmacokinetics'},
{q:'A man\'s phenytoin dose is raised from 300 mg to 350 mg a day. Two weeks later he is ataxic with nystagmus, and his level has more than doubled. Why?',o:['Its elimination is first-order, with a fixed half-life','It has induced its own hepatic metabolism over the two weeks','Its metabolism is saturated, so it is cleared at a fixed rate','The extra dose has displaced it from albumin'],a:2,x:'At therapeutic levels phenytoin\'s hepatic enzymes are close to saturation, so a fixed amount is cleared each day and any extra dose accumulates out of proportion (zero-order kinetics). Under first-order kinetics a 17% dose rise would raise the level by about 17%; displacement from albumin raises the free fraction but not the total level.',d:'pharm',c:'pharmacokinetics'},
{q:'A woman on stable warfarin is given clarithromycin for pneumonia. A week later her gums bleed. What happened to her INR, and why?',o:['It fell, because clarithromycin induces CYP2C9','It rose, because clarithromycin inhibits CYP3A4','It stayed the same; the bleeding is from thrombocytopenia','It fell, because clarithromycin binds vitamin K'],a:1,x:'Clarithromycin (like erythromycin) inhibits CYP3A4, slowing warfarin metabolism so its level and the INR rise; loss of vitamin K-producing gut flora adds to the effect. Azithromycin barely inhibits CYP3A4, which is why it is the safer macrolide with warfarin.',d:'pharm',c:'pharmacokinetics'},
{q:'A woman taking a combined oral contraceptive starts treatment for latent tuberculosis and becomes pregnant two months later. Which drug explains the contraceptive failure, and how?',o:['Rifampin, by inducing CYP3A4','Isoniazid, by inhibiting CYP enzymes','Pyrazinamide, by reducing absorption','Ethambutol, by increasing renal clearance'],a:0,x:'Rifampin is one of the strongest P450 inducers; it speeds metabolism of ethinylestradiol and progestins, so contraceptive levels fall and ovulation returns. Isoniazid is a P450 inhibitor and would raise, not lower, levels of other drugs. A copper or levonorgestrel intrauterine device avoids the interaction.',d:'pharm',c:'pharmacokinetics'},
{q:'A drug has a volume of distribution of 40 L and an oral bioavailability of 0.5. What oral loading dose gives a plasma level of 2 mg/L?',o:['40 mg','80 mg','320 mg','160 mg'],a:3,x:'Loading dose = target concentration × Vd / F = 2 × 40 / 0.5 = 160 mg. Forgetting to divide by bioavailability gives 80 mg, which would reach only half the target.',d:'pharm',c:'pharmacokinetics'},
{q:'Before starting carbamazepine for epilepsy in a woman of Han Chinese ancestry, which test is recommended, and what does it prevent?',o:['HLA-B*15:02, to prevent Stevens-Johnson syndrome','HLA-B*57:01, to prevent hypersensitivity','TPMT enzyme activity, to prevent marrow suppression','CYP2D6 genotype, to prevent liver failure'],a:0,x:'HLA-B*15:02, frequent in Han Chinese and other South-East Asian populations, sharply raises the risk of carbamazepine-induced Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis, so it is tested first. HLA-B*57:01 is the abacavir test, and TPMT predicts thiopurine toxicity.',d:'pharm',c:'toxicology'}
]};

BOOK['c6-pd']={depth:6,title:'The Locks and Keys',
epi:'Every key fits some lock in the dark. The question is how tightly, and what the lock does when it turns.',
c:['autonomic-drugs','g-proteins-toxins','digoxin'],
S:[
{h:'Dose and response',fig:'drc',p:[
'**Affinity** is how tightly a drug binds its receptor; **efficacy** (intrinsic activity) is how strongly the bound receptor responds. On a graded dose-response curve, **efficacy** is the maximum effect (the height of the curve) and **potency** is the amount needed for a given effect, shown by the **EC₅₀** (the position on the x-axis): a curve shifted left is more potent. A drug can be potent but have low efficacy, or the reverse. Clinically, efficacy usually matters more: a loop diuretic is more efficacious than a thiazide even if the thiazide is given in fewer milligrams.',
'A **full agonist** produces the maximum response. A **partial agonist** produces less than the maximum even at full occupancy (lower efficacy); in the presence of a full agonist it acts like an antagonist (buprenorphine with morphine, pindolol at β receptors). An **inverse agonist** reduces constitutive receptor activity (many H₁ antihistamines). Many tissues have **spare receptors**: a full response needs only a fraction of them occupied, so an irreversible antagonist may first shift the curve right before it finally lowers the maximum.'],
table:{cols:['Antagonist','Curve change','Example'],rows:[
['Competitive (reversible)','shifts the agonist curve right; same maximum; overcome by more agonist','naloxone on opioids, atropine on muscarinic receptors'],
['Non-competitive / irreversible','lowers the maximum; not overcome by more agonist','phenoxybenzamine on α receptors, organophosphates on acetylcholinesterase'],
['Partial agonist (given with a full agonist)','lowers the maximum toward its own ceiling','buprenorphine with morphine'],
['Physiological','opposes by a different receptor','glucagon vs insulin, adrenaline vs histamine in anaphylaxis'],
['Chemical','binds and inactivates the drug itself','protamine for heparin, digoxin Fab, chelators']]},
s:[['pharm','Therapeutic index = TD₅₀ / ED₅₀ (or LD₅₀ / ED₅₀ in animals): the safety margin, read from quantal dose-response curves that plot the fraction of a population responding. The therapeutic window is the range between the lowest effective level and the lowest toxic level. Narrow-index drugs need level monitoring: warfarin, digoxin, lithium, theophylline, phenytoin, aminoglycosides, vancomycin, ciclosporin and tacrolimus, carbamazepine.']]},
{h:'Four kinds of lock',p:[
'Receptors fall into four families, and their speed follows their mechanism. **Ligand-gated ion channels** (nicotinic acetylcholine, GABA-A, NMDA and AMPA glutamate, 5-HT₃) open a pore and act in milliseconds. **G-protein-coupled receptors** (seven transmembrane helices) act in seconds through second messengers. **Enzyme-linked receptors** act in minutes to hours by phosphorylating proteins. **Intracellular (nuclear) receptors** (steroids, thyroid hormone, vitamin D, retinoids) bind DNA as transcription factors and change gene expression over hours to days.',
'A GPCR works as a switch. The bound receptor makes its G protein swap GDP for GTP; the α subunit then leaves to act on its target until its own GTPase activity turns it off. Gs stimulates adenylate cyclase, Gi inhibits it, and Gq activates phospholipase C, which splits PIP₂ into **IP₃** (releases Ca²⁺ from the endoplasmic reticulum) and **DAG** (activates protein kinase C). Toxins that jam the switch, cholera toxin locking Gs on and pertussis toxin stopping Gi, show how much depends on that timer.'],
table:{cols:['G protein','Second messenger','Receptors'],rows:[
['Gs','↑adenylate cyclase → ↑cAMP → PKA','β₁, β₂, β₃, D₁, H₂, V₂, glucagon, PTH, TSH, ACTH, LH, FSH, hCG, CRH, GHRH, calcitonin'],
['Gi','↓cAMP','α₂, M₂, D₂, μ opioid, GABA-B, 5-HT₁'],
['Gq','phospholipase C → IP₃ (Ca²⁺) + DAG (PKC)','α₁, M₁, M₃, H₁, V₁, AT₁, 5-HT₂, GnRH, TRH, oxytocin, gastrin']]},
key:'Odd muscarinics raise calcium (M₁, M₃ on Gq); the even one brakes (M₂ on Gi). The α pair splits the same way: α₁ squeezes through Gq, α₂ brakes through Gi. Every β is Gs. H₁ and V₁ squeeze (Gq); H₂ and V₂ make cAMP (Gs) for acid and water.',
s:[['micro','Cholera toxin locks Gs on; pertussis toxin locks Gi off. Both raise cAMP.']]},
{h:'Locks that are enzymes',p:[
'Enzyme-linked receptors turn binding directly into phosphorylation. **Receptor tyrosine kinases** dimerise and phosphorylate each other when the ligand binds, then dock signalling proteins that start the RAS-MAPK and PI3K-AKT cascades; many cancer drugs block them. Cytokine receptors have no kinase of their own: they borrow a **Janus kinase (JAK)**, which phosphorylates STAT proteins that move to the nucleus. **Guanylate cyclase** receptors make cGMP, which activates protein kinase G and relaxes smooth muscle; natriuretic peptides bind a membrane form, while nitric oxide enters the cell and activates a soluble form. **Serine/threonine kinase** receptors for TGF-β signal through SMAD proteins.'],
table:{cols:['Receptor type','Ligands','Clinical link'],rows:[
['Receptor tyrosine kinase','insulin, IGF-1, EGF, PDGF, FGF, VEGF','insulin resistance; HER2, EGFR and VEGF inhibitors in cancer'],
['JAK-STAT (non-receptor tyrosine kinase)','growth hormone, prolactin, erythropoietin, thrombopoietin, G-CSF, interferons, most interleukins, leptin','JAK2 V617F in polycythaemia vera; JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, ruxolitinib)'],
['Membrane guanylate cyclase','ANP, BNP','natriuresis and vasodilation; sacubitril prolongs them'],
['Soluble guanylate cyclase','nitric oxide','nitrates; PDE5 inhibitors keep cGMP high (sildenafil)'],
['Serine/threonine kinase','TGF-β, BMPs, activin','fibrosis; SMAD4 loss in pancreatic cancer']]},
s:[['bio','cAMP and cGMP are broken down by phosphodiesterases. Blocking them prolongs the signal: theophylline (non-selective) in airways, milrinone (PDE3) in the failing heart, sildenafil (PDE5) in the corpus cavernosum and pulmonary arteries. Combining a PDE5 inhibitor with a nitrate stacks cGMP and can drop blood pressure dangerously.']]},
{h:'Tolerance and tachyphylaxis',p:[
'**Tolerance** develops over days to weeks (receptor down-regulation, metabolic adaptation): opioids, nitrates (need a nitrate-free interval), benzodiazepines, alcohol. At the receptor level, a heavily stimulated GPCR is phosphorylated by G-protein-coupled receptor kinases, β-arrestin binds it and uncouples it from its G protein, and the receptor is pulled inside the cell; with time, fewer receptors are made. **Tachyphylaxis** is fast loss of response with repeated doses: indirect sympathomimetics such as ephedrine and amphetamine deplete stored noradrenaline. **Sensitisation** and receptor up-regulation follow chronic antagonist use: stopping β-blockers abruptly can provoke angina or tachycardia, and stopping clonidine abruptly can cause rebound hypertension.'],
s:[['phys','Opioid tolerance spares miosis and constipation: pinpoint pupils persist even in long-term users.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'Drug A has an EC₅₀ of 1 mg and drug B an EC₅₀ of 10 mg; both reach the same maximum effect. Which statement is true?',o:['A is more efficacious than B','B is more potent than A','A is more potent than B','B is more efficacious than A'],a:2,x:'The lower EC₅₀ means drug A needs less to produce half its maximum effect, so A is more potent. Efficacy is the height of the curve, and both reach the same maximum, so they are equally efficacious.',d:'pharm',c:'autonomic-drugs'},
{q:'In an isolated muscle preparation, a fixed concentration of drug X is added before an agonist curve is repeated. The curve moves to the right, and very high agonist doses still reach the original maximum. What is drug X?',o:['A competitive reversible antagonist','An irreversible antagonist','A partial agonist at the same receptor','A physiological antagonist'],a:0,x:'A competitive antagonist occupies the receptor reversibly, so enough agonist outcompetes it: the curve shifts right with the same maximum. An irreversible antagonist removes receptors and lowers the maximum, and a partial agonist caps the response near its own ceiling.',d:'pharm',c:'autonomic-drugs'},
{q:'A man on high-dose morphine for cancer pain is given sublingual buprenorphine and within an hour develops yawning, sweating, diarrhoea and dilated pupils. Why?',o:['Buprenorphine is a full agonist that adds to morphine','Buprenorphine induces CYP3A4 and clears morphine','Buprenorphine binds irreversibly and permanently inactivates μ receptors','Buprenorphine is a high-affinity partial agonist at μ receptors'],a:3,x:'Buprenorphine binds μ receptors more tightly than morphine but activates them less, so it displaces the full agonist and the net opioid effect falls, precipitating withdrawal. It is not irreversible: its effect fades as it dissociates slowly, and it does not speed morphine metabolism.',d:'pharm',c:'substance-use'},
{q:'Phenylephrine drops make the radial muscle of the iris contract. Which signalling sequence follows binding to its receptor?',o:['Gs → adenylate cyclase → cAMP → protein kinase A','Gq → phospholipase C → IP₃ → Ca²⁺ release','Gi → less cAMP → opening of K⁺ channels','JAK → STAT → new gene transcription'],a:1,x:'Phenylephrine is an α₁ agonist, and α₁ receptors couple to Gq, which activates phospholipase C; IP₃ releases calcium from the endoplasmic reticulum and DAG activates protein kinase C, contracting smooth muscle. Gs and cAMP are the β pathway, which relaxes rather than contracts most smooth muscle.',d:'pharm',c:'autonomic-drugs'},
{q:'A man with stable angina uses long-acting isosorbide mononitrate twice daily and notices it working less after two weeks. What is the usual fix?',o:['Add a β-blocker and keep the nitrate unchanged','Double the dose and take it every eight hours','Give the last dose early to leave a nitrate-free interval','Switch to a PDE5 inhibitor'],a:2,x:'Continuous nitrate exposure causes tolerance through depleted sulfhydryl groups, oxidative stress and counter-regulatory neurohormones; an 8 to 12 hour nitrate-free interval each day, usually overnight, restores the effect. A higher dose deepens tolerance, and a PDE5 inhibitor with a nitrate can cause severe hypotension.',d:'pharm',c:'atherosclerosis'},
{q:'A 60-year-old has a haematocrit of 0.62, itching after hot baths and splenomegaly; his erythropoietin level is low. The mutated enzyme normally relays the signal from which kind of receptor?',o:['A receptor tyrosine kinase that phosphorylates itself','A G-protein-coupled receptor using cAMP','A nuclear receptor that binds DNA','A cytokine receptor that borrows a JAK kinase'],a:3,x:'Polycythaemia vera is driven by JAK2 V617F, which switches on the erythropoietin receptor pathway without the hormone, so red cells grow while erythropoietin falls. Erythropoietin, growth hormone and many cytokines use receptors with no kinase of their own that borrow a JAK; insulin and growth factors use true receptor tyrosine kinases.',d:'bio',c:'myeloma'}
]};

BOOK['c6-ans']={depth:6,title:'The Two Nerves',
epi:'Two wires run through every organ. One braces the body for flight. The other lets it rest and digest.',
c:['autonomic-drugs','catecholamines','nmj','parkinson'],
S:[
{h:'Wiring',p:[
'Both divisions use **acetylcholine on nicotinic (Nn) receptors** at their ganglia. **Parasympathetic** postganglionic fibres release **acetylcholine** on **muscarinic** receptors. **Sympathetic** postganglionic fibres release **noradrenaline** on **adrenergic** receptors, except sweat glands (acetylcholine on muscarinic receptors). The adrenal medulla is a modified ganglion that releases adrenaline into the blood. Skeletal muscle uses acetylcholine on **Nm** receptors. Most organs receive both inputs with opposite effects; the dominant tone at rest is parasympathetic in the heart, gut and bladder, and sympathetic in arterioles and veins, which is why ganglion blockade speeds the heart but drops blood pressure.',
'Catecholamines are built from tyrosine in a fixed order: tyrosine → DOPA (**tyrosine hydroxylase**, needs tetrahydrobiopterin; the rate-limiting step) → dopamine (**DOPA decarboxylase**, needs vitamin B6) → noradrenaline (**dopamine β-hydroxylase**, needs vitamin C and copper; it works inside vesicles) → adrenaline (**PNMT**, induced by cortisol arriving from the cortex, so it is concentrated in the adrenal medulla). Noradrenaline is removed mainly by reuptake through the noradrenaline transporter (NET, blocked by cocaine and TCAs) and broken down by MAO and COMT: noradrenaline to normetanephrine and vanillylmandelic acid (VMA), adrenaline to metanephrine and VMA, and dopamine to **homovanillic acid (HVA)**. Plasma free metanephrines screen for pheochromocytoma; urinary HVA and VMA are raised in neuroblastoma.'],
table:{cols:['Receptor','Main effects'],rows:[
['α₁','vasoconstriction, mydriasis (radial muscle), bladder sphincter contraction'],['α₂','presynaptic: ↓noradrenaline release; ↓insulin, ↓aqueous humour'],['β₁','↑heart rate, contractility, conduction; ↑renin'],['β₂','bronchodilation, vasodilation in muscle, uterine relaxation, ↑glycogenolysis, K⁺ into cells'],['β₃','detrusor relaxation, lipolysis'],
['M₁','CNS, enteric nerves'],['M₂','↓heart rate and conduction'],['M₃','glands (sweat, saliva, gastric acid), bronchoconstriction, bladder detrusor contraction, gut motility, miosis and accommodation (ciliary muscle)'],
['D₁','renal and mesenteric vasodilation'],['D₂','presynaptic inhibition; prolactin inhibition; CNS']]},
s:[['pharm','Every step at the nerve terminal is a drug target. At the **cholinergic** terminal, hemicholinium blocks the uptake of choline, the step that limits synthesis; vesamicol stops acetylcholine being loaded into vesicles; botulinum toxin cleaves the SNARE proteins needed for release; acetylcholinesterase inhibitors stop its breakdown in the cleft. At the **noradrenergic** terminal, metyrosine blocks tyrosine hydroxylase; reserpine blocks the vesicular monoamine transporter (VMAT) so stores drain away (and depression can follow); guanethidine and bretylium stop release; amphetamine and ephedrine push noradrenaline out through reversed transporters; cocaine and TCAs block reuptake; and α₂ autoreceptors, stimulated by clonidine, switch release off.']]},
{h:'Cholinergic drugs',p:[
'**Direct agonists**: bethanechol (urinary retention, ileus; resistant to cholinesterase, muscarinic only), carbachol and pilocarpine (glaucoma; pilocarpine also for dry mouth in Sjögren), methacholine (bronchial challenge test). **Acetylcholinesterase inhibitors**: neostigmine and pyridostigmine (myasthenia gravis, reversal of neuromuscular block; quaternary, so they do not cross into the brain), edrophonium (short-acting, historical diagnosis of myasthenia), physostigmine (tertiary, crosses into the brain; anticholinergic poisoning), donepezil, rivastigmine, galantamine (Alzheimer). Neostigmine is given with glycopyrrolate, an antimuscarinic that stays out of the brain, so the heart does not slow when the muscle block is reversed.',
'**Organophosphate poisoning** (insecticides, nerve agents) phosphorylates acetylcholinesterase and inactivates it, so acetylcholine floods every synapse. Picture the patient as **flooded and slowed**: every gland pours (tears, saliva, sweat, bronchial secretions), every hollow organ empties (vomiting, diarrhoea, urination), the airways tighten, the heart slows and the pupils shrink to points. At the nicotinic junctions of skeletal muscle there are fasciculations, then weakness and paralysis that can stop breathing. Treat with **atropine** (blocks the muscarinic flood; titrate until the chest is dry) and **pralidoxime** (pulls the phosphate off the enzyme and restores nicotinic function, but only before the bond "ages" and becomes permanent).'],
s:[['pharm','Antimuscarinics: atropine (bradycardia, organophosphates), ipratropium and tiotropium (COPD), oxybutynin and solifenacin (overactive bladder), scopolamine (motion sickness), benztropine and trihexyphenidyl (parkinsonism, drug-induced dystonia), tropicamide (pupil dilation). Poisoning gives the **dry furnace**: hot because sweating stops, dry skin and mouth, flushed skin as skin vessels open to shed heat, wide unreactive pupils and blurred near vision, delirium and hallucinations, and a full bladder with silent bowel. Jimsonweed seeds, diphenhydramine and TCAs cause the same picture.'],['pharm','Glaucoma drugs either cut aqueous production (β-blockers such as timolol, α₂ agonists such as brimonidine, carbonic anhydrase inhibitors such as acetazolamide and dorzolamide) or increase outflow (prostaglandin analogues such as latanoprost through the uveoscleral route, which can darken the iris; pilocarpine, which contracts the ciliary muscle and opens the trabecular meshwork). Antimuscarinics and α₁ agonists dilate the pupil and can precipitate acute angle-closure glaucoma.']]},
{h:'Adrenergic drugs',fig:'ans',p:[
'Predict any adrenergic drug from the receptors it touches. **α₁** constricts arterioles and veins, so pressure rises and the baroreceptor reflex slows the heart. **α₂** acts mainly in the brainstem and on nerve endings to cut sympathetic outflow, so pressure and heart rate fall. **β₁** speeds and strengthens the heart and releases renin. **β₂** relaxes bronchi, uterus and the arterioles of skeletal muscle, and drives potassium into cells. Low-dose adrenaline acts mostly on β receptors, so systolic pressure rises while diastolic pressure can fall; at high doses its α₁ action wins and both rise. Noradrenaline has little β₂ action, so pressure rises and the heart slows by reflex. Isoprenaline is pure β, so the heart races while diastolic pressure falls.',
'Pretreating with an α-blocker unmasks the β₂ effect of adrenaline: given afterwards, adrenaline lowers the blood pressure instead of raising it (adrenaline reversal). The same drug cannot reverse noradrenaline\'s pressor effect, because noradrenaline has almost no β₂ action to unmask. **Indirect sympathomimetics** act through the nerve terminal rather than the receptor: amphetamine releases stored noradrenaline and dopamine, ephedrine both releases noradrenaline and acts directly, cocaine and TCAs block reuptake, and dietary tyramine releases noradrenaline in people taking MAO inhibitors (the "cheese reaction").'],
table:{cols:['Drug','Receptors','Use'],rows:[
['Adrenaline','α₁, α₂, β₁, β₂ (β at low dose)','anaphylaxis, cardiac arrest, with local anaesthetics'],
['Noradrenaline','α₁ > β₁','septic shock (first-line); reflex bradycardia'],
['Isoprenaline','β₁ = β₂','bradyarrhythmias, torsades'],
['Dobutamine','β₁ > β₂','cardiogenic shock, stress echocardiography'],
['Dopamine','D₁ (low), β₁ (medium), α₁ (high)','shock, bradycardia'],
['Phenylephrine','α₁','hypotension, nasal decongestant, pupil dilation'],
['Salbutamol, salmeterol, formoterol','β₂','asthma and COPD (long-acting β₂ agonists only with an inhaled steroid in asthma)'],
['Mirabegron, vibegron','β₃','overactive bladder (relaxes detrusor)'],
['Clonidine, α-methyldopa, brimonidine','α₂ agonists','hypertension (methyldopa in pregnancy), ADHD, opioid and alcohol withdrawal, glaucoma (brimonidine)'],
['Amphetamine, ephedrine, cocaine','indirect: release noradrenaline or block its reuptake','ADHD and narcolepsy (amphetamine); decongestant (ephedrine); cocaine causes vasospasm, so avoid a β-blocker alone in cocaine chest pain'],
['Prazosin, terazosin, doxazosin, tamsulosin','α₁ blockers','BPH, hypertension; first-dose hypotension; prazosin for PTSD nightmares'],
['Phenoxybenzamine, phentolamine','α blockers (irreversible / reversible)','pheochromocytoma before surgery; cocaine or MAOI crisis; phentolamine for extravasated noradrenaline'],
['Mirtazapine','α₂ antagonist','depression with poor sleep and appetite'],
['β-blockers','β₁ selective: atenolol, metoprolol, bisoprolol, esmolol, nebivolol (also releases nitric oxide); non-selective: propranolol, nadolol, timolol; mixed α₁/β: carvedilol, labetalol; partial agonists with intrinsic sympathomimetic activity: pindolol, acebutolol (avoided after MI)','angina, heart failure (metoprolol succinate, bisoprolol, carvedilol), arrhythmias, migraine, thyrotoxicosis, varices, essential tremor']]},
s:[['pharm','In pheochromocytoma, give an α-blocker before a β-blocker: unopposed α stimulation can cause a hypertensive crisis. β-blocker toxicity: bradycardia, hypotension, hypoglycaemia; give glucagon, which raises cAMP in the heart without needing β receptors. β-blockers mask hypoglycaemic tremor and tachycardia (sweating, which is cholinergic, remains).'],['phys','A pure α₁ agonist raises pressure and triggers baroreceptor reflex bradycardia; a pure β₂ agonist lowers pressure and causes reflex tachycardia.']]}
],
Q:[
{q:'A farmer is brought in after spraying crops: pinpoint pupils, wheezing, drooling, vomiting, a heart rate of 42 and twitching muscles. Which drugs should be given first?',o:['Physostigmine and diazepam','Atropine and pralidoxime','Naloxone and oxygen','Adrenaline and fluids'],a:1,x:'Organophosphates inactivate acetylcholinesterase, flooding muscarinic and nicotinic synapses; atropine blocks the muscarinic flood and pralidoxime reactivates the enzyme before ageing, which also restores muscle function. Physostigmine is another cholinesterase inhibitor and would worsen the poisoning, and naloxone reverses opioids, which also cause small pupils but not secretions or fasciculations.',d:'pharm',c:'autonomic-drugs'},
{q:'A woman with pneumonia remains hypotensive with cold peripheries after 30 mL/kg of crystalloid; her lactate is 5 mmol/L. Which vasopressor is first-line?',o:['Dopamine','Phenylephrine','Isoprenaline','Noradrenaline'],a:3,x:'In septic shock that persists after fluids, noradrenaline is first-line: its α₁ action restores vascular tone and its β₁ action supports the heart, with fewer arrhythmias than dopamine. Phenylephrine raises pressure with pure α₁ action but can lower cardiac output, and isoprenaline would worsen vasodilation through β₂.',d:'pharm',c:'shock'},
{q:'A man with paroxysmal headaches, sweating and blood pressure of 210/120 has raised plasma metanephrines and an adrenal mass. Which drug should be started first before surgery?',o:['Phenoxybenzamine','Propranolol','Esmolol','Hydrochlorothiazide'],a:0,x:'α-blockade with phenoxybenzamine (or doxazosin) comes first, for 1 to 2 weeks, to control pressure and let the contracted plasma volume expand. Giving a β-blocker first removes β₂ vasodilation and leaves catecholamines acting on α₁ receptors unopposed, which can cause a hypertensive crisis.',d:'pharm',c:'catecholamines'},
{q:'A 70-year-old woman has urge incontinence with frequent small voids. Oxybutynin helps but gives her dry mouth and confusion. Oxybutynin acts by blocking which receptor, and what is an alternative with a different mechanism?',o:['α₁ on the sphincter; tamsulosin','M₂ on the detrusor; bethanechol','β₂ on the detrusor; salbutamol','M₃ on the detrusor; mirabegron'],a:3,x:'Oxybutynin blocks M₃ receptors on the detrusor, and its central antimuscarinic effects can cause confusion in older people. Mirabegron relaxes the detrusor through β₃ receptors, avoiding antimuscarinic side effects. Bethanechol is a muscarinic agonist used for urinary retention, the opposite problem.',d:'pharm',c:'autonomic-drugs'},
{q:'A teenager is hot, dry and flushed, with dilated pupils, a heart rate of 140, a full bladder and visual hallucinations after eating seeds from a roadside weed. Which antidote crosses into the brain to reverse this?',o:['Atropine','Pralidoxime','Physostigmine','Flumazenil with naloxone'],a:2,x:'Jimsonweed contains antimuscarinic alkaloids, and physostigmine, a tertiary cholinesterase inhibitor, crosses the blood-brain barrier and reverses both the central delirium and the peripheral signs. Neostigmine would reverse only the peripheral signs because it is quaternary, and atropine would make the poisoning worse.',d:'pharm',c:'autonomic-drugs'},
{q:'A patient given a drug for hypertension in the 1960s became severely depressed. The drug blocks the transporter that loads monoamines into vesicles, so stores of noradrenaline, dopamine and serotonin drain away. Which drug?',o:['Guanethidine','Reserpine','Metyrosine','Clonidine'],a:1,x:'Reserpine blocks VMAT, so monoamines left in the cytoplasm are destroyed by MAO and stores empty, lowering pressure but also causing depression and parkinsonism. Guanethidine blocks release rather than storage, metyrosine blocks synthesis at tyrosine hydroxylase, and clonidine reduces outflow through α₂ receptors.',d:'pharm',c:'catecholamines'},
{q:'In an experiment, an animal is first given phentolamine and then adrenaline. What happens to its blood pressure, and why?',o:['It rises more, because β₁ effects are unmasked','It does not change, because all receptors are blocked','It falls, because β₂ vasodilation is unmasked','It rises normally, because phentolamine is reversible'],a:2,x:'With α receptors blocked, adrenaline can act only on β receptors, and β₂ dilation of muscle arterioles lowers total peripheral resistance, so pressure falls (adrenaline reversal). Noradrenaline has little β₂ action, so the same pretreatment simply blunts its pressor effect rather than reversing it.',d:'phys',c:'autonomic-drugs'}
]};

BOOK['c6-tox']={depth:6,title:'The Poison Wells',
epi:'Some currents pool into wells. Learn which antidote clears which well before you drink.',
c:['toxicology','free-radicals','oxphos','ethanol','g6pd'],
S:[
{h:'Antidotes',p:[
'Most poisonings need supportive care first: airway, breathing, circulation, glucose. Then think in three steps. **Decontamination**: activated charcoal adsorbs many drugs if given within about an hour of ingestion, but not metals (iron, lithium, lead), alcohols, acids and alkalis or hydrocarbons; whole-bowel irrigation flushes iron, lithium, packets of drugs and sustained-release tablets. **Enhanced elimination**: alkalinising the urine traps salicylate; haemodialysis removes small, water-soluble, weakly bound drugs with a low volume of distribution (salicylates, lithium, toxic alcohols, metformin, valproate, theophylline). **Antidotes**: a short list of specific reversals.'],
table:{cols:['Poison','Antidote'],rows:[
['Paracetamol (acetaminophen)','N-acetylcysteine'],['Opioids','naloxone'],['Benzodiazepines','flumazenil (risk of seizures in chronic users)'],['Anticholinergics','physostigmine'],['Organophosphates','atropine, pralidoxime'],
['β-blockers','glucagon'],['Calcium channel blockers','calcium, high-dose insulin with glucose'],['Digoxin','digoxin-specific Fab fragments'],['Tricyclic antidepressants','sodium bicarbonate (wide QRS)'],
['Heparin','protamine sulfate'],['Warfarin','vitamin K, prothrombin complex concentrate'],['Dabigatran','idarucizumab'],['Rivaroxaban, apixaban','andexanet alfa'],['tPA, streptokinase','tranexamic acid'],
['Methanol, ethylene glycol','fomepizole (or ethanol), dialysis'],['Cyanide','hydroxocobalamin; nitrites + thiosulfate'],['Carbon monoxide','100% or hyperbaric oxygen'],['Methaemoglobin (nitrites, dapsone, benzocaine)','methylene blue (not in G6PD deficiency)'],
['Iron','deferoxamine'],['Lead','succimer (children), dimercaprol + EDTA (severe)'],['Arsenic, mercury','dimercaprol, succimer'],['Copper (Wilson)','penicillamine, trientine'],['Isoniazid','pyridoxine'],['Salicylates','sodium bicarbonate (alkalinise urine), dialysis'],['Methotrexate','leucovorin (folinic acid)'],['Local anaesthetics (bupivacaine)','20% lipid emulsion; benzodiazepines for seizures'],['Sulfonylureas','glucose, then octreotide (stops further insulin release)'],['Lithium','IV fluids, haemodialysis if severe'],['Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity','IV lipid emulsion'],['Hydrofluoric acid','calcium gluconate (topical, local injection or IV)'],['Malignant hyperthermia','dantrolene'],['Neuroleptic malignant syndrome','stop the drug, cooling, dantrolene, bromocriptine'],['Serotonin syndrome','stop the drug, benzodiazepines, cyproheptadine']]}},
{h:'Reading the toxidrome',p:[
'Before any level comes back, the bedside signs usually name the class of poison. Look at the pupils, feel the skin, count the breaths and the pulse, take the temperature and listen to the bowel. Two contrasts settle most cases: **sweat** separates the sympathomimetic patient (wet) from the anticholinergic one (dry), and **pupil size with breathing** separates the opioid patient (pinpoint, slow) from the sedative-hypnotic one (normal pupils, near-normal breathing with benzodiazepines alone).'],
table:{cols:['Toxidrome (examples)','Pupils and skin','Vital signs and other signs'],rows:[
['**Sympathomimetic**: cocaine, amphetamines, MDMA','dilated; sweaty','↑HR, ↑BP, ↑temperature; agitation, seizures, chest pain'],
['**Anticholinergic**: atropine, diphenhydramine, TCAs, jimsonweed','dilated; hot, dry, flushed','↑HR, ↑temperature; delirium, urinary retention, silent bowel'],
['**Cholinergic**: organophosphates, carbamates, nerve agents','pinpoint; wet','↓HR; secretions, bronchospasm, vomiting, diarrhoea, fasciculations, weakness'],
['**Opioid**: heroin, fentanyl, methadone','pinpoint; normal or cool','↓respiratory rate, ↓consciousness; quiet bowel; naloxone reverses'],
['**Sedative-hypnotic**: benzodiazepines, barbiturates, alcohol','normal; normal','drowsy or comatose; breathing near normal with benzodiazepines alone, depressed with barbiturates or mixtures'],
['**Serotonin syndrome**: SSRIs with MAOIs, linezolid, tramadol, MDMA','dilated; sweaty','↑HR, ↑temperature; clonus and hyperreflexia (legs more than arms), diarrhoea; onset within hours'],
['**Neuroleptic malignant syndrome**: antipsychotics, stopping levodopa','normal; sweaty','↑temperature, labile BP; lead-pipe rigidity, slowed reflexes, very high CK; onset over days']]},
key:'Wet and wide: sympathomimetic. Dry and wide: anticholinergic. Wet and tiny: cholinergic. Tiny and barely breathing: opioid.'},
{h:'Paracetamol and salicylates',p:[
'Paracetamol is normally conjugated (glucuronidation and sulfation), with a small fraction oxidised by CYP2E1 to **NAPQI**, which glutathione detoxifies. In overdose the conjugation pathways saturate, more drug goes to NAPQI, glutathione runs out and NAPQI binds hepatocyte proteins, killing cells in **zone 3** (centrilobular), where CYP2E1 is richest and oxygen poorest. Chronic alcohol use (which induces CYP2E1) and starvation (which lowers glutathione) increase the risk. Early symptoms are mild nausea; transaminases climb after a day and liver failure peaks at 3 to 4 days.',
'**N-acetylcysteine** supplies cysteine to rebuild glutathione and can also bind NAPQI directly. It is almost fully protective when started within 8 hours, and it still helps later, even in established liver failure. The need for it is judged by plotting the paracetamol level taken at least 4 hours after a single ingestion on the treatment nomogram.',
'**Salicylate** overdose stimulates the respiratory centre first (**respiratory alkalosis**), then uncouples oxidative phosphorylation and adds organic acids (**anion gap metabolic acidosis**): a mixed picture in adults. Tinnitus, vomiting, hyperthermia, confusion and non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema. Treat with **sodium bicarbonate** to trap salicylate in alkaline urine and keep it out of the brain; dialysis if severe (confusion, kidney failure, pulmonary oedema or very high levels).'],
s:[['pharm','Aspirin in children with viral illness can cause Reye syndrome: microvesicular fatty liver and encephalopathy, from mitochondrial injury.']]},
{h:'Toxic alcohols and gases',p:[
'**Methanol** (windscreen fluid, illicit spirits) is metabolised by alcohol dehydrogenase to formaldehyde and then formic acid, which poisons the optic nerve: blurred "snowstorm" vision progressing to blindness, and basal ganglia necrosis. **Ethylene glycol** (antifreeze) becomes glycolic and oxalic acid: calcium oxalate crystals, flank pain and acute kidney injury, hypocalcaemia. Both cause a high **anion gap and osmolar gap** acidosis. **Fomepizole** blocks alcohol dehydrogenase so the harmless parent alcohol is excreted unchanged; dialysis removes both the alcohol and its acids. **Isopropyl alcohol** raises the osmolar gap without acidosis (it becomes acetone, a ketone that is not an acid).',
'**Carbon monoxide** (fires, faulty heaters, car exhaust) binds haemoglobin about 240 times more tightly than oxygen and shifts the dissociation curve to the left, so less oxygen is carried and what is carried is held back; it also blocks cytochrome oxidase. Headache, nausea and confusion; cherry-red skin is a late sign. The PaO₂ is normal, and pulse oximetry reads falsely normal because it counts carboxyhaemoglobin as oxyhaemoglobin; true saturation and carboxyhaemoglobin are measured by co-oximetry. **Cyanide** (house fires, nitroprusside, industrial salts) binds the ferric iron of cytochrome oxidase (complex IV), so cells cannot use oxygen: lactic acidosis, venous blood that stays bright red, collapse; hydroxocobalamin binds it to form cyanocobalamin. **Methaemoglobinaemia**: iron oxidised to Fe³⁺ cannot carry oxygen; chocolate-coloured blood, cyanosis unresponsive to oxygen, pulse oximetry stuck near 85% with a normal PaO₂; methylene blue speeds its reduction using NADPH.'],
s:[['bio','Disulfiram and metronidazole block aldehyde dehydrogenase: acetaldehyde builds up after alcohol (flushing, nausea, palpitations). East Asian ALDH2 variants cause the same flushing.'],['bio','Lead inhibits ALA dehydratase and ferrochelatase, so ALA and zinc protoporphyrin build up and haem synthesis fails: a microcytic anaemia with basophilic stippling and ring sideroblasts. It also causes abdominal colic, wrist or foot drop from motor neuropathy, a blue line on the gums, dense lines at the growing ends of children\'s bones, and encephalopathy in children exposed to old paint or contaminated water.']]},
{h:'Drug reactions to recognise',p:[
'Adverse reactions fall into two kinds. **Predictable** reactions are exaggerations of a drug\'s known actions, depend on dose, and can happen to anyone (bleeding on warfarin, hypoglycaemia on insulin). **Idiosyncratic** reactions do not follow the drug\'s pharmacology, are often immune-mediated, and depend on the patient (HLA type, enzyme variants), so they are rare but tend to be severe and to recur with re-exposure. Many are tested by pattern: a drug list linked to one organ, so learn the organ and the usual culprits together.'],
table:{cols:['Reaction','Drugs'],rows:[
['Stevens-Johnson syndrome, toxic epidermal necrolysis','sulfonamides, lamotrigine, carbamazepine, phenytoin, allopurinol, nevirapine'],
['Drug-induced lupus','hydralazine, procainamide, isoniazid, minocycline, TNF inhibitors; anti-histone antibodies (anti-dsDNA usually absent, though TNF inhibitors can induce it); kidneys and CNS usually spared; resolves when the drug stops'],
['QT prolongation','class IA and III antiarrhythmics, macrolides, fluoroquinolones, antipsychotics (haloperidol), methadone, ondansetron, TCAs'],
['Agranulocytosis','clozapine, carbamazepine, propylthiouracil, methimazole, dapsone, sulfasalazine'],
['Haemolysis in G6PD deficiency','primaquine, dapsone, sulfonamides, nitrofurantoin, rasburicase, fava beans'],
['Pulmonary fibrosis','bleomycin, busulfan, amiodarone, methotrexate, nitrofurantoin'],
['Gingival hyperplasia','phenytoin, ciclosporin, verapamil and nifedipine'],
['Photosensitivity','tetracyclines, amiodarone, sulfonamides, fluoroquinolones, thiazides'],
['Gynaecomastia','spironolactone, ketoconazole, cimetidine, digoxin, oestrogens, GnRH analogues'],
['Ototoxicity','aminoglycosides, loop diuretics, cisplatin, vancomycin'],
['Acute interstitial nephritis','NSAIDs, penicillins and cephalosporins, proton pump inhibitors, rifampin, diuretics'],
['Tendinopathy and tendon rupture','fluoroquinolones (more with steroids and in older people)'],
['Haemorrhagic cystitis','cyclophosphamide, ifosfamide (mesna protects)'],
['Dilated cardiomyopathy','doxorubicin (dose-dependent; dexrazoxane protects), trastuzumab (usually reversible)'],
['Osteonecrosis of the jaw','bisphosphonates, denosumab'],
['Tardive dyskinesia','antipsychotics, metoclopramide'],
['Disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol','metronidazole, some cephalosporins, procarbazine, first-generation sulfonylureas'],
['Red man syndrome','vancomycin (rate-related histamine release; slow the infusion)']]}}
],
Q:[
{q:'A man found confused beside an empty bottle of antifreeze has a pH of 7.08, an anion gap of 28, an osmolar gap of 30 and envelope-shaped crystals in his urine. What is the best treatment?',o:['Intravenous N-acetylcysteine infusion','Hydroxocobalamin','Fomepizole and haemodialysis','Naloxone and supportive care'],a:2,x:'Alcohol dehydrogenase turns ethylene glycol into glycolic and oxalic acid, giving a high anion gap, a high osmolar gap from the unmetabolised alcohol and calcium oxalate crystals that injure the tubules. Fomepizole blocks the enzyme and dialysis removes the glycol and its acids. N-acetylcysteine is for paracetamol, and hydroxocobalamin is for cyanide, which causes lactic acidosis without an osmolar gap.',d:'pharm',c:'toxicology'},
{q:'A woman has ringing in her ears, vomiting and rapid breathing after an overdose. Her blood gas shows a respiratory alkalosis together with a high anion gap metabolic acidosis. What is the key treatment?',o:['Ammonium chloride to acidify the urine','Sodium bicarbonate to alkalinise the urine','Flumazenil to reverse the sedation','Atropine to dry the airway secretions'],a:1,x:'Salicylate first stimulates the respiratory centre and then uncouples oxidative phosphorylation, giving this mixed disorder; bicarbonate ionises the weak acid, trapping it in urine and keeping it out of the brain. Acidifying the urine would do the opposite and increase reabsorption; dialysis is added for severe poisoning.',d:'pharm',c:'acid-base'},
{q:'After topical benzocaine spray for an endoscopy, a man becomes cyanosed. His blood is chocolate-brown, pulse oximetry reads 85% on high-flow oxygen, and his PaO₂ is 320 mmHg. What is the antidote?',o:['Methylene blue','Hydroxocobalamin','Deferoxamine','Vitamin K'],a:0,x:'Benzocaine oxidises haemoglobin iron to Fe³⁺, and methaemoglobin cannot carry oxygen, so the patient is cyanosed despite a high PaO₂; methylene blue speeds its reduction back to Fe²⁺ using NADPH. Hydroxocobalamin treats cyanide, in which haemoglobin is normal but cells cannot use oxygen.',d:'pharm',c:'toxicology'},
{q:'A woman who drinks heavily takes 30 paracetamol tablets. Three days later she is jaundiced with an ALT over 5000 U/L. Which part of the liver lobule is destroyed first, and why there?',o:['Zone 1, because it receives the most toxin from the portal blood','Bile ducts, because the toxin is excreted in bile','Zone 2, because it has the most mitochondria','Zone 3, because it has the most CYP2E1 and the least oxygen'],a:3,x:'Zone 3 around the central vein has the highest CYP2E1 activity, which makes NAPQI, and the lowest oxygen, so it dies first; chronic alcohol use induces CYP2E1 and adds to the risk. Zone 1 near the portal tracts is hit first by toxins that need no activation, such as yellow phosphorus, and in viral hepatitis.',d:'path',c:'toxicology'},
{q:'A man treated for epilepsy for several years has swollen, overgrown gums, coarse facial features and a mild macrocytic anaemia. Which drug is most likely?',o:['Phenytoin','Lithium','Amiodarone','Isoniazid'],a:0,x:'Phenytoin causes gingival hyperplasia, coarse facies, hirsutism and a megaloblastic anaemia through interference with folate. Ciclosporin and calcium channel blockers also cause gingival overgrowth, but lithium, amiodarone and isoniazid do not.',d:'pharm',c:'excitability'},
{q:'A man is agitated with a temperature of 39.6 °C, a heart rate of 150, blood pressure of 190/110, dilated pupils and skin drenched with sweat. Bowel sounds are active. Which class of poison is most likely?',o:['An antimuscarinic such as diphenhydramine','An opioid such as fentanyl','An organophosphate insecticide','A sympathomimetic such as cocaine'],a:3,x:'Dilated pupils, tachycardia, hypertension and hyperthermia with drenching sweat point to a sympathomimetic, because sweat glands are sympathetic but cholinergic and still work. An antimuscarinic gives the same pupils and pulse but hot dry skin and a silent bowel; opioids and organophosphates shrink the pupils.',d:'pharm',c:'toxicology'},
{q:'A woman taking procainamide for an arrhythmia develops arthralgia, fever and a pleural effusion. Her kidneys are normal. Which antibody is most characteristic?',o:['Anti-double-stranded DNA','Anti-histone','Anti-Smith','Anti-centromere'],a:1,x:'Drug-induced lupus from slow acetylation of procainamide or hydralazine gives anti-histone antibodies, joint and serosal disease, and usually spares the kidneys and brain; it resolves when the drug stops. Anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith point to idiopathic SLE, which more often involves the kidneys.',d:'path',c:'autoimmunity'}
]};
